WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Tesla Energy's three gigawatt-hour storage deal with zero shows grid scale demand growing independently of vehicle sales. This is Tesla Shorts Time, episode 591. An Italian firm has committed to three gigawatt hours of Tesla Energy Storage capacity. The agreement targets utility scale megapack projects across European grids. It adds directly to the Tesla Energy backlog without depending on vehicle sales cycles. The commitment reflects rising demand for grid storage

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:57.360
that supports renewable integration. Contracts like this give the energy business a steadier revenue profile than retail vehicle demand. While energy storage shows independent momentum, Tesla is also expanding factory space that could support multiple product lines at once. Construction at gigatexas now includes dedicated areas for humanoid robot production. The site is also adding space for a chip fabrication facility focused on inference hardware.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:20.140
These expansions align with earlier plans to scale non-vehicle hardware output. The facility must still balance resources across vehicles, robots, and energy products. Dedicated zones should help reduce internal competition for floor space and engineering time. Factory growth supports future hardware, but real world vehicle testing is already underway in Canada.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:40.920
Winnipeg-based carrier Bison Transport has begun real world testing of the Tesla semi. The trucks are running routes that include Chicago area legs, early data collection focuses on range, charging times, and payload compared with diesel trucks. Fleet operators want to see how the electric semi performs in daily commercial service.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:02:07.400
Results from these runs will help determine whether the truck months long haul economics. While the semi faces practical testing supervised FSD continues to add small interface changes. A new navigation feature now integrates FSD speed profile data. The system adjusts driving behavior based on route information without manual input. Owners have spotted the change in the navigation interface on recent software versions.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:18.340
It adds context-aware adjustments on top of existing supervised FSD capabilities. It remains unclear whether the feature will expand beyond the initial vehicles where it appeared.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:34.800
Software refinements aim to improve daily use, yet voice control is also expanding in parallel. Grog handled 116 of 170 voice commands in a recent test. The commands covered climate control, navigation, media, and vehicle settings.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:56.080
All tasks were completed using only the hey Grog wake phrase and no screen interaction. The test demonstrates expanding hands-free capability inside the vehicle. Results show the system can manage a wide range of common driver requests. While in-car features improve, regulatory and safety questions continue to surface in multiple markets.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:16.880
A second fatal crash involved a stationary Tesla with autopilot or supervised FSD active. The incident occurred in Mesa while the vehicle was stopped on a freeway. Authorities are investigating the collision, which follows a similar earlier case. The event raises questions about how the system detects and responds to stopped vehicles.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:28.880
Investigations like this keep attention on driver assistance performance in edge situations. Safety incidents draw scrutiny and Australian courts are considering deeper technical reviews of Tesla systems.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:40.640
An Australian judge has proposed giving a court-appointed expert access to Tesla engineering systems. The move aims to resolve disputes over driver assistance features in ongoing litigation.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:49.080
Tesla has not yet responded publicly to the suggestion. Independent review of the vehicle systems could clarify technical details for the court.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:04:04.820
Legal questions continue in Australia where local regulators are also examining a new in-car game feature. Experts in Australia have raised concerns about a new self-driving game feature. They warned the game could distract drivers during supervised operation.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:13.880
Regulators are being urged to review the feature before any wider rollout. Criticism focuses on how the game integrates with existing vehicle controls.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:24.660
The discussion highlights ongoing questions about secondary tasks, all driver assistance is active. Regulatory friction exists alongside steady delivery volume reaching Europe.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:37.160
The vessel Glovis Nova arrived at the port of Antwerp Bruges carrying roughly 8,000 Tesla vehicles. The large shipment reflects ongoing European delivery flows through established maritime routes.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:46.840
Similar loads indicate steady production output from North American and European factories. The volume supports continued supply to multiple European markets.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:57.820
Deliveries remain robust, while Tesla also advances retail plans in Australia. Tesla is moving forward with plans for a new showroom and service facility in Jolong.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:11.040
The site would provide dedicated sales and service capacity for the Australian market. Local planning approvals are progressing for the project. The expansion forms part of broader efforts to grow Tesla presence in Australia.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:21.120
Retail growth pairs with customer-driven ideas for improving the charging experience. Owners have suggested an on-screen system to show supercharger queue position.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:31.260
The proposal would display a driver's place in line and notify them when a stall opens. Current random parking at busy sites sometimes leads to disputes over order.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:41.020
The idea aims to reduce friction without requiring new hardware at the stations. Community discussion shows practical interest in smoother charging site operations.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:50.900
Every day usability also shows up in long-term vehicle reviews. An Australian long-term test of the model Y highlights software and interface quality.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:06:01.120
The review notes that over-the-air updates continue to change daily usability. Technology stands out more than traditional metrics such as range or dynamics in the right up.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:10.420
The piece positions software as a key differentiator in the Australian market. Real-world durability data is emerging alongside these software strings.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:24.660
A three-year-old model Y structural battery pack reached 100,000 miles. The owner tracked degradation and reported better than expected longevity. Most charging was at lower speeds, with only occasional fast charging sessions.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:34.160
The data adds to real-world records for structural pack performance. High mileage examples like this help build confidence in long-term battery behavior.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:44.420
Now one thing worth watching is a recent low-speed incident in Hong Kong. A Tesla struck a student near a school on the first day of classes, injuring the child's foot.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:53.020
The event occurred at low speed and is under local review. It adds to questions about supervised system behavior in pedestrian dense areas.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:07:05.420
Cases like this keep attention on edge-case handling near schools and other sensitive zones. Energy storage contracts like the recent zero agreement expose a basic difference between vehicle and grid businesses.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:16.140
Vehicle demand fluctuates with consumer sentiment, incentives, and model cycles, while utility-scale storage responds to grid reliability needs and renewable integration targets.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:24.680
The three gigawatt-hour commitment sits outside those swings and is priced on capacity and cycle life rather than range or acceleration.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:34.140
Tesla's vertical integration in cells and power electronics gives it a cost path that pure-play storage competitors must match through scale or partnerships.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:42.420
Yet the same integration also ties capital allocation across vehicle lines, robotaxi ambitions, and optimist development.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:52.140
When a single factory complex must serve multiple product families, any delay in one program can shift cash and engineering attention away from storage output.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:08:02.440
The practical result is that Tesla energy margins depend less on selling more cars and more on how quickly megapack production can be decoupled from vehicle line constraints.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:10.940
Before we go, watch for more details on how bison transports semi-testing results compare with diesel performance on those Chicago routes.

